The base `Capsule` contains only a digest for `memory`.

The open observer can inspect and constrain the shared Capsule fields while allowing the virtual field to change without understanding its contents. The concrete `Agent` contract receives the `AgentMemory` preimage, verifies it against the digest, exposes its typed fields, and repacks the updated object into the next digest.
[/quote]

Note: the compiler generates the deserialization logic verifying the supplied preimage against the stored digest, then and decodes according to the concrete layout. Here is the generated `.sil`:
```
entrypoint function step(/*...*/, byte[8] gen__memory_agent_memory_preimage) {
  require(blake2b(gen__memory_agent_memory_preimage) == memory);
  int gen__memory_hunger = OpBin2Num(gen__memory_agent_memory_preimage.slice(0, 8));

  /* ... */
}
```

I've been working on adapting KCC20 to build upon @IzioDev's KCC1 & KCC2. This raises a few challenges.

Authenticating different principal types requires variable-length parameters. KCC20 currently defines the leader `transfer` entrypoint as the one responsible for authenticating every input. This raises a technical issue: the entrypoint needs to receive an array of bytes which might have a different length for each input.

A possible solution is to move the authentication responsibility to each of the delegating inputs, and away from the leader.

This results in another issue with borrowing. The borrowed input needs to validate the borrow itself, or make sure that the leader will validate it on its behalf.

I propose a solution which restricts borrowing to only the leading input, possibly as a new entrypoint. This would result in 3 entrypoints for each token:

```
// Delegation entrypoint receives a variable-length owner pk preimage
// and validates its own authentication
transfer_delegator(sig signature, byte[] owner_opening)

// Borrow entrypoint, which expects the first output in the state transition to be
// the borrow-continuation with an increased amount, and validates the state
// transition and amount preservation
borrow(State[] next_states)

// A transfer entrypoint which both validates the leader input authentication
// and validates the state transition and amount preservation
transfer(State[] next_states, sig signature, byte[] owner_opening)

```

